From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: aquarat <3090997+aquarat@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 15:12:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] KVM: arm64: Handle Apple PAuth virtualization state under VHE

macOS guests on QEMU's vmapple machine (the platform of Apple's
Virtualization.framework) run with Apple's pointer authentication
extension. The guest manages its keys through vendor hypercalls, which the
VMM serves by writing the guest's key registers with KVM_SET_ONE_REG. KVM
already handles the architectural keys, but not Apple's registers:

- APCTL (control) and KERNKEYLO/HI (the kernel key) are per-vCPU EL1
  state, reached from a VHE hypervisor through their EL12 aliases;
- APVMKEYLO/HI_EL2 hold a VM key that the guest's effective keys depend
  on, used when APSTS_EL12 is 1.

Without them a macOS guest cannot get past its key setup.

Detection: a new capability, ARM64_HAS_PAUTH_VIRT_APPLE, matches the M1
and M2 parts (t8103, t6000/1/2, t8112, t6020/1/2) by MIDR, on every early
CPU, with the kernel at EL2. No ID register advertises these registers, a
probe would fault on a CPU without them, and later parts lock them: m1n1's
per-CPU table marks the Apple system registers unlocked on M1, M2 and M3
but not on M4 and later. The list is the M1 and M2 rows of that table (M3
is left out until KVM runs there). The existing ACTLR virtualization
capabilities are not used: ARM64_HAS_ACTLR_VIRT matches every Apple part
from M2 on, including the locked ones.

KVM uses it only in VHE mode and only on bare metal: a static key,
kvm_has_apple_pauth(), is enabled at init if the capability is present,
has_vhe() is true and there is no SMCCC conduit. A guest hypervisor under
nested virtualization also runs at (virtual) EL2 and sees the host's MIDR,
but there these registers UNDEF; Apple machines have no firmware below
EL2, so finding a conduit means a hypervisor is below us. In nVHE, hVHE and
protected mode, and on every other CPU, nothing changes: no new register
accesses and no new registers visible to userspace.

State: APCTL and KERNKEYLO/HI are new vCPU sysregs that reset to 0. Like
the other EL1 registers under VHE they live in the CPU while the vCPU is
loaded: __vcpu_load_switch_sysregs() writes them through the EL12 aliases
and __vcpu_put_switch_sysregs() reads them back. They are written on every
load, so a vCPU never runs with another vCPU's or the host's values.

VM key: each VM gets a random 128-bit key from get_random_bytes() when it
is created. Every vcpu_load writes it to APVMKEYLO/HI_EL2 and sets
APSTS_EL12 to 1 before the guest's keys, so all vCPUs of a VM run with the
same key for the VM's life, and no vCPU runs under another VM's key. The
key is not exposed to userspace and is cleared when the VM is destroyed.
The host runs at EL2 and does not use these registers, so they are not
restored on put.

Guest memory signed under one VM's key cannot be resumed in a different
VM. On M1 Max that applies to the keys a guest writes itself while Apple
mode (APCTL bit 0) is on, which the VM key diversifies: two VMs with
identical guest state then sign differently, so such a guest migrated, or
restored from a snapshot, into a new VM (with a new key) would fail
pointer authentication. Keys set through KVM_SET_ONE_REG, which is how
QEMU's vmapple support serves macOS's key hypercalls, and guests that
leave APCTL at 0 (Linux) gave the same signatures in every VM. QEMU's
vmapple machine refuses savevm (its pflash is not snapshot-capable) but
does not block migration unless the GPU proxy device is present.

Userspace: KVM_GET_ONE_REG, KVM_SET_ONE_REG and KVM_GET_REG_LIST handle
the 64-bit registers ARM64_SYS_REG(3, 6, 15, 15, 0) (APCTL) and
ARM64_SYS_REG(3, 6, 15, 2, 3/4) (KERNKEYLO/HI), the EL12 encodings that
QEMU's vmapple support uses. Elsewhere they stay unknown registers.

Guest accesses: HCR_EL2.TIDCP traps the guest's accesses to IMPDEF
registers. The three registers above UNDEF through their sys_reg_descs
entries. The EL1 forms, APSTS and the VM key have no entry and UNDEF on
the existing path for unknown IMPDEF registers, so the guest can neither
read nor change the VM key or APSTS.

On M1 Max every guest EL1 access to these encodings (EL1 forms, EL12
aliases, APSTS, APVMKEYLO/HI_EL2, APCTL_EL2) traps to KVM as a SYS64
exception and gets UNDEF; guest EL0 accesses UNDEF without trapping.

Anees Iqbal (steelbrain) first showed that macOS guests under KVM need
this state
(https://github.com/steelbrain/experiment-macOS-arm64-on-asahi-linux-arm64).
This is an independent implementation, written from m1n1's register
definitions and a functional specification without access to that code.

Tested on t6001 (M1 Max), Asahi asahi-wip (Linux 7.2.11) with Fedora's
7.2.11-200 patch and configs: macOS 26.4 guests (4, 6 and 8 vCPUs; the
simulator unit-test workload), a macOS 13.6 guest, a macOS 26 and a macOS
13 guest at once on the same four CPUs, a Linux guest's own pointer
authentication and the host's pointer authentication.

Signed-off-by: aquarat <3090997+aquarat@users.noreply.github.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/arm64/include/asm/apple_cpufeature.h | 19 ++++++++++
 arch/arm64/include/asm/kvm_host.h         | 23 ++++++++++++
 arch/arm64/kernel/cpufeature_impdef.c     | 36 +++++++++++++++++++
 arch/arm64/kvm/arm.c                      | 28 +++++++++++++++
 arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/vhe/sysreg-sr.c        | 43 +++++++++++++++++++++++
 arch/arm64/kvm/sys_regs.c                 | 21 +++++++++++
 arch/arm64/tools/cpucaps                  |  1 +
 7 files changed, 171 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/include/asm/apple_cpufeature.h b/arch/arm64/include/asm/apple_cpufeature.h
index 4370d91ff..f24907db7 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/include/asm/apple_cpufeature.h
+++ b/arch/arm64/include/asm/apple_cpufeature.h
@@ -12,4 +12,23 @@
 #define ACTLR_APPLE_TSO_SHIFT	1
 #define ACTLR_APPLE_TSO		BIT(1)
 
+/*
+ * Apple pointer authentication extension (M1/M2), encodings as in m1n1.
+ * EL1 registers, and their EL12 aliases for a VHE hypervisor:
+ */
+#define SYS_IMP_APL_APCTL_EL1		sys_reg(3, 4, 15, 0, 4)
+#define SYS_IMP_APL_KERNKEYLO_EL1	sys_reg(3, 4, 15, 1, 0)
+#define SYS_IMP_APL_KERNKEYHI_EL1	sys_reg(3, 4, 15, 1, 1)
+#define SYS_IMP_APL_APSTS_EL1		sys_reg(3, 6, 15, 12, 4)
+#define SYS_IMP_APL_APCTL_EL12		sys_reg(3, 6, 15, 15, 0)
+#define SYS_IMP_APL_KERNKEYLO_EL12	sys_reg(3, 6, 15, 2, 3)
+#define SYS_IMP_APL_KERNKEYHI_EL12	sys_reg(3, 6, 15, 2, 4)
+#define SYS_IMP_APL_APSTS_EL12		sys_reg(3, 6, 15, 14, 7)
+/* EL2 only: the VM key, which a guest's effective keys depend on */
+#define SYS_IMP_APL_APVMKEYLO_EL2	sys_reg(3, 6, 15, 14, 4)
+#define SYS_IMP_APL_APVMKEYHI_EL2	sys_reg(3, 6, 15, 14, 5)
+
+/* APSTS_EL12: select the virtualized PAuth state for the guest */
+#define APSTS_APPLE_VM_KEY		BIT(0)
+
 #endif
diff --git a/arch/arm64/include/asm/kvm_host.h b/arch/arm64/include/asm/kvm_host.h
index a49042bfa..2e9362d10 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/include/asm/kvm_host.h
+++ b/arch/arm64/include/asm/kvm_host.h
@@ -414,6 +414,12 @@ struct kvm_arch {
 	/* Count the number of VNCR_EL2 currently mapped */
 	atomic_t vncr_map_count;
 
+	/*
+	 * Apple PAuth VM key (APVMKEYLO/HI_EL2), random per VM, used by all
+	 * vCPUs for the VM's life. Only set if kvm_has_apple_pauth().
+	 */
+	u64 apple_vmkey[2];
+
 	/*
 	 * For an untrusted host VM, 'pkvm.handle' is used to lookup
 	 * the associated pKVM instance in the hypervisor.
@@ -490,6 +496,11 @@ enum vcpu_sysreg {
 	APGAKEYLO_EL1,
 	APGAKEYHI_EL1,
 
+	/* Apple IMPDEF pointer authentication (kvm_has_apple_pauth()) */
+	APPLE_APCTL_EL1,	/* PAuth control */
+	APPLE_KERNKEYLO_EL1,	/* Kernel key, low */
+	APPLE_KERNKEYHI_EL1,	/* Kernel key, high */
+
 	/* Memory Tagging Extension registers */
 	RGSR_EL1,	/* Random Allocation Tag Seed Register */
 	GCR_EL1,	/* Tag Control Register */
@@ -1443,6 +1454,18 @@ static inline bool kvm_system_needs_idmapped_vectors(void)
 	return cpus_have_final_cap(ARM64_SPECTRE_V3A);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Apple's PAuth state is only handled under VHE on bare metal, where KVM
+ * owns EL2 and reaches the guest's copy through the EL12 encodings. nVHE,
+ * hVHE and protected mode never see it. Set once at init.
+ */
+DECLARE_STATIC_KEY_FALSE(kvm_apple_pauth);
+
+static inline bool kvm_has_apple_pauth(void)
+{
+	return static_branch_unlikely(&kvm_apple_pauth);
+}
+
 void kvm_init_host_debug_data(void);
 void kvm_debug_init_vhe(void);
 void kvm_vcpu_load_debug(struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu);
diff --git a/arch/arm64/kernel/cpufeature_impdef.c b/arch/arm64/kernel/cpufeature_impdef.c
index aee7571fb..5f9cf8f0a 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/kernel/cpufeature_impdef.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/cpufeature_impdef.c
@@ -7,6 +7,7 @@
 
 #include <asm/cpufeature.h>
 #include <asm/apple_cpufeature.h>
+#include <asm/virt.h>
 #include <linux/irqflags.h>
 #include <linux/preempt.h>
 #include <linux/printk.h>
@@ -76,6 +77,35 @@ static bool has_apple_actlr_virt(const struct arm64_cpu_capabilities *entry, int
 	return midr >= MIDR_APPLE_M2_BLIZZARD && midr <= MIDR_CPU_MODEL(ARM_CPU_IMP_APPLE, 0xfff);
 }
 
+/*
+ * The Apple pointer authentication registers that a hypervisor needs to
+ * run guests (APCTL, KERNKEY, APSTS, APVMKEY) are accessible at EL2 on M1
+ * and M2. No ID register advertises them, so match the parts by MIDR.
+ * This is the M1 and M2 part of m1n1's table of CPUs whose Apple system
+ * registers are unlocked; t6002 and t6022 report t6001 and t6021 parts.
+ */
+static const struct midr_range apple_pauth_virt_cpus[] = {
+	MIDR_ALL_VERSIONS(MIDR_APPLE_M1_ICESTORM),
+	MIDR_ALL_VERSIONS(MIDR_APPLE_M1_FIRESTORM),
+	MIDR_ALL_VERSIONS(MIDR_APPLE_M1_ICESTORM_PRO),
+	MIDR_ALL_VERSIONS(MIDR_APPLE_M1_FIRESTORM_PRO),
+	MIDR_ALL_VERSIONS(MIDR_APPLE_M1_ICESTORM_MAX),
+	MIDR_ALL_VERSIONS(MIDR_APPLE_M1_FIRESTORM_MAX),
+	MIDR_ALL_VERSIONS(MIDR_APPLE_M2_BLIZZARD),
+	MIDR_ALL_VERSIONS(MIDR_APPLE_M2_AVALANCHE),
+	MIDR_ALL_VERSIONS(MIDR_APPLE_M2_BLIZZARD_PRO),
+	MIDR_ALL_VERSIONS(MIDR_APPLE_M2_AVALANCHE_PRO),
+	MIDR_ALL_VERSIONS(MIDR_APPLE_M2_BLIZZARD_MAX),
+	MIDR_ALL_VERSIONS(MIDR_APPLE_M2_AVALANCHE_MAX),
+	{},
+};
+
+static bool has_apple_pauth_virt(const struct arm64_cpu_capabilities *entry, int scope)
+{
+	return is_kernel_in_hyp_mode() &&
+	       is_midr_in_range_list(apple_pauth_virt_cpus);
+}
+
 static const struct arm64_cpu_capabilities arm64_impdef_features[] = {
 #ifdef CONFIG_ARM64_MEMORY_MODEL_CONTROL
 	{
@@ -108,6 +138,12 @@ static const struct arm64_cpu_capabilities arm64_impdef_features[] = {
 		.type = ARM64_CPUCAP_EARLY_LOCAL_CPU_FEATURE,
 		.matches = has_apple_actlr_virt,
 	},
+	{
+		.desc = "PAuth virtualization (IMPDEF, Apple)",
+		.capability = ARM64_HAS_PAUTH_VIRT_APPLE,
+		.type = ARM64_CPUCAP_EARLY_LOCAL_CPU_FEATURE,
+		.matches = has_apple_pauth_virt,
+	},
 	{},
 };
 
diff --git a/arch/arm64/kvm/arm.c b/arch/arm64/kvm/arm.c
index 00df8783c..b19afe51e 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/kvm/arm.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kvm/arm.c
@@ -21,6 +21,8 @@
 #include <linux/irqbypass.h>
 #include <linux/sched/stat.h>
 #include <linux/psci.h>
+#include <linux/random.h>
+#include <linux/string.h>
 #include <trace/events/kvm.h>
 
 #define CREATE_TRACE_POINTS
@@ -275,6 +277,10 @@ int kvm_arch_init_vm(struct kvm *kvm, unsigned long type)
 
 	bitmap_zero(kvm->arch.vcpu_features, KVM_VCPU_MAX_FEATURES);
 
+	if (kvm_has_apple_pauth())
+		get_random_bytes(kvm->arch.apple_vmkey,
+				 sizeof(kvm->arch.apple_vmkey));
+
 	return 0;
 
 err_uninit_mmu:
@@ -337,6 +343,8 @@ void kvm_arch_destroy_vm(struct kvm *kvm)
 	kvm_unshare_hyp(kvm, kvm + 1);
 
 	kvm_arm_teardown_hypercalls(kvm);
+
+	memzero_explicit(kvm->arch.apple_vmkey, sizeof(kvm->arch.apple_vmkey));
 }
 
 static bool kvm_has_full_ptr_auth(void)
@@ -2993,6 +3001,24 @@ void kvm_arch_irq_bypass_start(struct irq_bypass_consumer *cons)
 	kvm_arm_resume_guest(irqfd->kvm);
 }
 
+DEFINE_STATIC_KEY_FALSE(kvm_apple_pauth);
+
+static void __init kvm_apple_pauth_init(void)
+{
+	/*
+	 * The capability matches the CPU by MIDR at EL2, which a guest
+	 * hypervisor (nested virt) also sees, but there these registers UNDEF.
+	 * Apple machines have no firmware below EL2, so bare metal has no
+	 * SMCCC conduit, while a hypervisor below us provides one.
+	 */
+	if (!has_vhe() || !cpus_have_final_cap(ARM64_HAS_PAUTH_VIRT_APPLE) ||
+	    arm_smccc_1_1_get_conduit() != SMCCC_CONDUIT_NONE)
+		return;
+
+	static_branch_enable(&kvm_apple_pauth);
+	kvm_info("Apple PAuth virtualization state enabled\n");
+}
+
 /* Initialize Hyp-mode and memory mappings on all CPUs */
 static __init int kvm_arm_init(void)
 {
@@ -3052,6 +3078,8 @@ static __init int kvm_arm_init(void)
 	if (err)
 		goto out_hyp;
 
+	kvm_apple_pauth_init();
+
 	kvm_info("%s%sVHE%s mode initialized successfully\n",
 		 in_hyp_mode ? "" : (is_protected_kvm_enabled() ?
 				     "Protected " : "Hyp "),
diff --git a/arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/vhe/sysreg-sr.c b/arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/vhe/sysreg-sr.c
index be685b63e..02a5929cc 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/vhe/sysreg-sr.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/vhe/sysreg-sr.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 #include <linux/compiler.h>
 #include <linux/kvm_host.h>
 
+#include <asm/apple_cpufeature.h>
 #include <asm/kprobes.h>
 #include <asm/kvm_asm.h>
 #include <asm/kvm_emulate.h>
@@ -198,6 +199,44 @@ static inline void __mpam_guest_load(void)
 	}
 }
 
+/*
+ * Apple PAuth: the guest's APCTL and kernel key live in the CPU while the
+ * vcpu is loaded, like its other EL1 registers. The VM key and APSTS are
+ * not guest state: they are written on every load, before the guest's
+ * keys, so that a CPU never runs a vcpu under another VM's key. The host
+ * runs at EL2 and doesn't use any of them, so nothing is restored on put.
+ */
+static void __apple_pauth_load(struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu)
+{
+	struct kvm_cpu_context *ctxt = &vcpu->arch.ctxt;
+	const u64 *vmkey = vcpu->kvm->arch.apple_vmkey;
+
+	if (!kvm_has_apple_pauth())
+		return;
+
+	write_sysreg_s(vmkey[0], SYS_IMP_APL_APVMKEYLO_EL2);
+	write_sysreg_s(vmkey[1], SYS_IMP_APL_APVMKEYHI_EL2);
+	write_sysreg_s(APSTS_APPLE_VM_KEY, SYS_IMP_APL_APSTS_EL12);
+	/* Their effect on the key registers isn't documented: order them */
+	isb();
+
+	write_sysreg_s(ctxt_sys_reg(ctxt, APPLE_APCTL_EL1), SYS_IMP_APL_APCTL_EL12);
+	write_sysreg_s(ctxt_sys_reg(ctxt, APPLE_KERNKEYLO_EL1), SYS_IMP_APL_KERNKEYLO_EL12);
+	write_sysreg_s(ctxt_sys_reg(ctxt, APPLE_KERNKEYHI_EL1), SYS_IMP_APL_KERNKEYHI_EL12);
+}
+
+static void __apple_pauth_save(struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu)
+{
+	struct kvm_cpu_context *ctxt = &vcpu->arch.ctxt;
+
+	if (!kvm_has_apple_pauth())
+		return;
+
+	ctxt_sys_reg(ctxt, APPLE_APCTL_EL1) = read_sysreg_s(SYS_IMP_APL_APCTL_EL12);
+	ctxt_sys_reg(ctxt, APPLE_KERNKEYLO_EL1) = read_sysreg_s(SYS_IMP_APL_KERNKEYLO_EL12);
+	ctxt_sys_reg(ctxt, APPLE_KERNKEYHI_EL1) = read_sysreg_s(SYS_IMP_APL_KERNKEYHI_EL12);
+}
+
 /**
  * __vcpu_load_switch_sysregs - Load guest system registers to the physical CPU
  *
@@ -257,6 +296,8 @@ void __vcpu_load_switch_sysregs(struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu)
 		__sysreg_restore_el1_state(guest_ctxt, midr, mpidr);
 	}
 
+	__apple_pauth_load(vcpu);
+
 	vcpu_set_flag(vcpu, SYSREGS_ON_CPU);
 }
 
@@ -283,6 +324,8 @@ void __vcpu_put_switch_sysregs(struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu)
 	else
 		__sysreg_save_el1_state(guest_ctxt);
 
+	__apple_pauth_save(vcpu);
+
 	__sysreg_save_user_state(guest_ctxt);
 	__sysreg32_save_state(vcpu);
 
diff --git a/arch/arm64/kvm/sys_regs.c b/arch/arm64/kvm/sys_regs.c
index fa5c93c7a..6f660e98b 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/kvm/sys_regs.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kvm/sys_regs.c
@@ -19,6 +19,7 @@
 #include <linux/uaccess.h>
 #include <linux/irqchip/arm-gic-v3.h>
 
+#include <asm/apple_cpufeature.h>
 #include <asm/arm_pmuv3.h>
 #include <asm/cacheflush.h>
 #include <asm/cputype.h>
@@ -1531,6 +1532,22 @@ static unsigned int ptrauth_visibility(const struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu,
 	__PTRAUTH_KEY(k ## KEYLO_EL1),					\
 	__PTRAUTH_KEY(k ## KEYHI_EL1)
 
+static unsigned int apple_pauth_visibility(const struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu,
+					   const struct sys_reg_desc *rd)
+{
+	return kvm_has_apple_pauth() ? 0 : REG_HIDDEN;
+}
+
+/*
+ * The guest's Apple PAuth state, for userspace: a VMM serves the guest's
+ * key management hypercalls with KVM_SET_ONE_REG on the EL12 encodings.
+ * The guest itself can't access it: if its access traps, it UNDEFs, as
+ * do the EL1 forms, APSTS and the VM key, which have no entry here.
+ */
+#define APPLE_PAUTH_REG(enc, r)						\
+	{ SYS_DESC(SYS_IMP_APL_ ## enc), undef_access, reset_val, r, 0,	\
+	  .visibility = apple_pauth_visibility }
+
 static bool access_arch_timer(struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu,
 			      struct sys_reg_params *p,
 			      const struct sys_reg_desc *r)
@@ -3880,6 +3897,10 @@ static const struct sys_reg_desc sys_reg_descs[] = {
 	{ SYS_DESC(SYS_CNTV_CVAL_EL02), access_arch_timer },
 
 	EL2_REG(SP_EL2, NULL, reset_unknown, 0),
+
+	APPLE_PAUTH_REG(KERNKEYLO_EL12, APPLE_KERNKEYLO_EL1),
+	APPLE_PAUTH_REG(KERNKEYHI_EL12, APPLE_KERNKEYHI_EL1),
+	APPLE_PAUTH_REG(APCTL_EL12, APPLE_APCTL_EL1),
 };
 
 static bool handle_at_s1e01(struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu, struct sys_reg_params *p,
diff --git a/arch/arm64/tools/cpucaps b/arch/arm64/tools/cpucaps
index de92d63fd..3f6a90705 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/tools/cpucaps
+++ b/arch/arm64/tools/cpucaps
@@ -55,6 +55,7 @@ HAS_MOPS
 HAS_NESTED_VIRT
 HAS_BBML2_NOABORT
 HAS_PAN
+HAS_PAUTH_VIRT_APPLE
 HAS_PMUV3
 HAS_S1PIE
 HAS_S1POE
-- 
